The frequency models stay inside their tables when a tone is not a note

A tone is a note plus the bend and whatever an instrument and a channel add to
it, so it reaches well below 0 and well above 127. Seven of the eight frequency
models take it as it comes:

* oplModel_dmxFreq(), oplModel_apogeeFreq(), oplModel_9xFreq() and
  oplModel_OConnellFreq() convert the tone to an unsigned type, which is
  undefined for a tone below zero:

    src/models/model_apogee.c:75:13: runtime error: -128 is outside the range of
    representable values of type 'unsigned long'

  oplModel_genericFreq() already holds the tone at zero first, and these do it
  the same way. In oplModel_9xFreq() that was also the whole of a second report,
  since the note it made out of the tone went on to shift by more than the width
  of a word.

* oplModel_ailFreq() adds an octave offset that counts how far below the table
  the note was, and shifts the result into the block field, which is undefined
  when the sum is negative:

    src/models/model_ail.c:156:27: runtime error: left shift of negative
    value -1

  oplModel_hmiFreq() holds the octave at zero for this, with the comment "Don't
  wrap everything to top!"; this does the same, in the same place.

* oplModel_msAdLibFreq() adds a half-tone offset that a downward bend makes
  negative, and then reads the frequency table at that note and shifts its
  octave:

    src/models/model_msadlib.c:111:12: runtime error: index -1 out of bounds for
    type 'uint16_t[12]'

  The lowest note is as far down as it goes.

* s_hmi_bend_calc() holds an index at the size of the table it reads, which is
  one past its last entry:

    src/models/model_hmi_sos.c:102:19: runtime error: index 103 out of bounds
    for type 'uint_fast32_t[103]'
    AddressSanitizer: global-buffer-overflow

  The note that the bend looks one step above is 114, the highest the caller
  allows, so an ordinary high note is enough to read past the end. The last
  entry of a table is its size less one.

The first of these came out of a fuzz target of ADLplug-Next that plays MIDI
through the real-time API, with the emulator and the volume model that a saved
project can hold; the daily run found it in a quarter of an hour. The rest came
from calling each model over the range of tones that a note, a bend and an
instrument's offsets can make, from -128 to 256, under the address and undefined
sanitizers.

OPL3::noteOn() holds a tone below zero at zero -- "Lower than 0 is impossible!"
-- so the four conversions want a caller that does not. The models are a library
of their own, and opl_models.h offers every one of these functions, with the tone
as a double, to whoever includes it.

Over the notes themselves -- 0 to 127, every sixteenth of a tone, 2048 points
for each of the eight models -- the answer changes at 210 of the 16384, and at
every one of them the old answer came out of the fault. oplModel_ailFreq() at
the tones below 12, where the octave went negative: a tone of 0 answered
0xfeb2, the sign of the octave standing in the frequency, and now answers
0x02b2. oplModel_msAdLibFreq() at 84 tones of the lowest octave, the ones
whose bend takes the note below zero and the read off the front of the table.
s_hmi_bend_calc() at the tones of 114 and 126, where it read past the end. The
other 16174 answers are the same byte for byte.

This is ADLplug-Next's own patch, applied to the submodule by
cmake/Patches.cmake when CMake configures, and it goes away once the change is
upstream.

diff --git a/src/models/model_ail.c b/src/models/model_ail.c
index abb5513..014acf1 100644
--- a/src/models/model_ail.c
+++ b/src/models/model_ail.c
@@ -147,6 +147,9 @@ uint16_t oplModel_ailFreq(double tone, uint32_t *mul_offset)
 
     octave += octaveOffset;
 
+    if(octave < 0)
+        octave = 0; /* Don't wrap everything to top! */
+
     while(octave > 7)
     {
         ++(*mul_offset);
diff --git a/src/models/model_apogee.c b/src/models/model_apogee.c
index 94d4122..cf9c895 100644
--- a/src/models/model_apogee.c
+++ b/src/models/model_apogee.c
@@ -72,6 +72,9 @@ uint16_t oplModel_apogeeFreq(double tone, uint32_t *mul_offset)
 
     *mul_offset = 0;
 
+    if(tone < 0.0)
+        tone = 0.0;
+
     noteI = (uint_fast32_t)(tone >= 12 ? tone - 12 : tone);
     bendDec = tone - (int)tone;
 
diff --git a/src/models/model_dmx.c b/src/models/model_dmx.c
index 0eeee04..6bb50b9 100644
--- a/src/models/model_dmx.c
+++ b/src/models/model_dmx.c
@@ -147,6 +147,9 @@ uint16_t oplModel_dmxFreq(double tone, uint32_t *mul_offset)
 
     *mul_offset = 0;
 
+    if(tone < 0.0)
+        tone = 0.0;
+
     if(tone >= 12)
         tone -= 12;
 
diff --git a/src/models/model_hmi_sos.c b/src/models/model_hmi_sos.c
index 9eac2f2..893720e 100644
--- a/src/models/model_hmi_sos.c
+++ b/src/models/model_hmi_sos.c
@@ -55,7 +55,7 @@ static uint_fast32_t s_hmi_bendtable[12] =
         0 : \
         ( \
             (formula) >= (int32_t)maxVal ? \
-            (int32_t)maxVal : \
+            (int32_t)maxVal - 1 : \
             (formula) \
         )\
     )
diff --git a/src/models/model_msadlib.c b/src/models/model_msadlib.c
index 5e35d78..9f6ed19 100644
--- a/src/models/model_msadlib.c
+++ b/src/models/model_msadlib.c
@@ -108,6 +108,10 @@ uint16_t oplModel_msAdLibFreq(double tone, uint32_t *mul_offset)
     }
 
     note += halfToneoffset;
+
+    if(note < 0)
+        note = 0; /* Don't go below the lowest note! */
+
     freq = s_msAdLibFreqTable[delta][note % 12];
     octave = note / 12;
 
diff --git a/src/models/model_oconnell.c b/src/models/model_oconnell.c
index 640bce5..7cbff4e 100644
--- a/src/models/model_oconnell.c
+++ b/src/models/model_oconnell.c
@@ -64,6 +64,9 @@ uint16_t oplModel_OConnellFreq(double tone, uint32_t *mul_offset)
 
     *mul_offset = 0;
 
+    if(tone < 0.0)
+        tone = 0.0;
+
     note = (uint_fast32_t)tone;
     bendDec = tone - (int)tone;
 
diff --git a/src/models/model_win9x.c b/src/models/model_win9x.c
index 2ec5f9d..1b86d5d 100644
--- a/src/models/model_win9x.c
+++ b/src/models/model_win9x.c
@@ -64,6 +64,9 @@ uint16_t oplModel_9xFreq(double tone, uint32_t *mul_offset)
 
